    "data": {
      "A": "Research low-risk improvements to state-table lookup and allocation. First benchmark using the cached hash as a filter before `memcmp` in the existing chained table; measure collision and lookup costs before considering table changes. The open-addressing variant measured 0.006285 s versus the 0.004945 s baseline, so do not assume a different table is faster. Preserve exact state equality and BFS behavior.",
      "B": "Investigate move-generation occupancy checks using per-parent scratch data (for example, mark box cells once, then check the four candidate moves), while retaining the canonical sorted box coordinates used in state keys. Compare against the current linear scan across varied valid boards; do not assume binary search or insertion changes help—the measured sorted-insertion variant was 0.004984 s, slightly slower than baseline, and the full-scan reduction variant was 0.006209 s. Keep candidate generation and state admission in the baseline order.",
      "C": "Evaluate whether parallel move checks can amortize their allocation, synchronization, and commit costs on this 4-core ARM64 target. The tested batch/OpenMP approach measured 0.006346 s, substantially slower than baseline; retain a serial path and test any activation threshold with repeated end-to-end measurements. Ensure boundary checks precede all board indexing, and commit results in the authoritative BFS order so shortest-path output is not changed.",
      "G": "Integrate only independently measured, correctness-checked changes. Start from the valid 0.004945 s baseline; all listed variants were slower in this run, with the nearest at 0.004984 s. Test combinations as complete programs rather than adding individual speedups, since interactions may worsen performance. Verify output against the reference on the full correctness set, including tied shortest solutions and board boundaries, then compare repeated mean runtimes; retain baseline behavior if no reliable gain appears.",
      "analysis": "The task is exact shortest-solution Sokoban BFS; performance changes must preserve valid-input correctness, canonical state equality, and observable path ordering. In the supplied source, `do_move` enqueues R,L,U,D, while the statement describes U,D,L,R tie order; treat this discrepancy as an explicit correctness question and reconcile it with the authoritative oracle before changing search order. Baseline measurement: 0.004945 s, valid and correct on 4 cases. Measured alternatives: sorted-box insertion 0.004984 s; neighbor table plus binary-search occupancy and insertion 0.005160 s; occupancy reduction 0.006209 s; open addressing 0.006285 s; batched OpenMP 0.006346 s. These measurements show no demonstrated speedup, so prioritize focused experiments and reproducibility over speculative integration."
